00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.110 align:start position:0%
 
main  independent advisor on ministers  interests lord guyt has resigned he also  released a brief statement  which mentioned with regret i feel that  it is right that i'm resigning from my  post as independent advisor on ministers  interests of course this has been  something that has been debated for  quite some time it did feel like it had  gone away that the prime minister  perhaps was  if not safe not in peril with this  record with this potential resignation  um and of course this uh revolves around  the story as to whether  uh the prime minister gave a sufficient  explanation um as to why or why he  hadn't uh broken uh the  ministerial code  and this has been going back and forth  and openly discussed and he then given  another response to lord guide as to why  he felt he had not broken the minnesota  code or he'd given the sufficient  explanation lord geit now resigning is a  big big moment it's a big extra amount  of pressure on the prime minister who  may well have felt that he uh did escape  this particular threat to his future and  leadership our political respondent uh  liz bates able to join me now from uh  westminster with reaction to this and uh  and and liz uh perhaps this was expected  or a possibility a few weeks ago but the  expectation of whether this would  materialize or not had had gone away  somewhat  it had and it's quite interesting uh  that we've just heard this in the last  few minutes because we heard from lord  got yesterday he was speaking in front  of a committee of mps and we didn't  really have a hint at that point that he  was thinking about stepping down  although we knew that he had been  frustrated this has really come the  combination of um something that came  out last month which was a sort of  public standoff  over  partygate lord geith is of course the  prime minister's advisor on ethics and i  think he was quite frustrated with the  prime minister over this issue of the  fixed penalty notice that he had  received over party gate and whether  that equated to a breach of the  ministerial code this is a position that  he set out in an annual report that he  released and what came across very  strongly in that report that was that  what his frustration with the prime  minister uh that as as the arbiter of  the ministerial code he hadn't explained  publicly why he thought that that fixed  penalty notice wasn't a breach of that  but as i said uh yes he appeared  yesterday in front of a committee of mps  and we didn't get any sense at that  point that he was going um to step down  even though there had been rumors  circulating around that but today  just in the last few minutes we have had  a statement uh from lord geit he says  with regret i feel that it is right the  time resigning from my post as  independent advisor on ministers  interests so clear now that after  everything that's happened he feels uh  that his uh position is untenable and  that he has had to stand down today and  uh beyond that we don't really know yet  uh what's gone on uh behind the scenes  but clearly he feels he cannot go on in  his position as the prime minister's  advisor on ethics so interesting list  because there's been so many moving  parts of course to the prime minister's  position within his own party over  recent weeks he has just survived a  confidence vote uh and as the rules  stand today that means he can't face  another confidence vote within his own  party for another 12 months this  resignation  is only really something that could put  pressure on him within those realms is  it is it not and again  if it come moments before the vote of  confidence maybe it would have swayed a  few mps votes but in the very short term  does it make an immediate impact  well that's a really interesting  question and i think what you've touched  upon there is that there is has been  frustration uh brewing for a long time  um particularly over partygate but other  issues as well within the conservative  party and there have been many moments  where it has felt like the prime  minister's position was becoming  untenable and lord guys speaking out on  this issue was one of those moments i  think i've been speaking to conservative  mps uh  all the time about how they feel about  the prime minister and as you said one  of those  one of the key  points is that the cannot now be another  confidence vote in him and a lot of  those mps that are very disillusioned in  the prime minister are feeling at the  moment that like there isn't an  opportunity to remove him even though  we still keep having moments like this  resignations and clearly people losing  confidence in the prime minister but as  i say conservative mp's feeling that at  the moment he's untouchable and even  though things like this are happening  it's still possible for him to continue  in his position

